it was her first public comment since  leaving office last year tonight here in  berlin former german chancellor angela  merkel  described the war in ukraine as  quoting here a huge tragedy  she defended her policy towards russia  over her 16 years as chancellor she said  she does not blame herself  for the events that are unfolding now  but  she did admit  she says she asked herself if she could  have done more to prevent the war from  happening  here is part of what she had to say  about how the conflict has been  impacting her life  is  my retirement hasn't turned out quite  the way i'd expected  february 24th was a major turning point  and it's something i think about often  but if you're asking about my personal  feelings  i stepped down for my position as  chancellor by choice  and that's a nice feeling  i've started doing other things  and that also feels nice  but at heart i remain a political person  so at times like these i feel as worried  as many others do  all right let's go now to our chief  political editor michaela kufner she was  there listening to that  interview tonight nicola you know so  many people here in germany but around  the world they've been waiting for  angela merkel to break her silence since  retirement what did she have to say  well first of all she said it wasn't a  silence that after 16  years in office and six months without  making public statements isn't such a  long time she condemned this russian  aggression she also described ukraine as  a hostage in the standoff between  vladimir putin and the west that he was  attacking everything that western  liberal democracy  stands for and that he was attacking the  very foundations upon which a peaceful  togetherness rests she said that she  wanted to find a way  if if one cannot be friends with russia  at least of coexistence and that  diplomacy failing doesn't mean that one  shouldn't have tried now we're all  asking yourselves why she never picked  up the phone to vladimir putin let's  take a listen to what she had to say  about that  what i find particularly calming is that  if something were to happen something i  thought was being handled in entirely  the wrong way while there's any number  of people i could call but that's not  been necessary  so basically she's been saying she would  not call vladimir putin unless she was  asked by the current government who she  felt was doing were professionals she  described them as after all shots the  current chancellor was her vice  chancellor and that she didn't wouldn't  do anything unless being asked by him by  the government at the very least she  doesn't seem to see it as her role to  interfere and we also learned from her  who's been in parallel with vladimir  putin for way more than a decade in  office that her feeling was that the  only language he understands anyway  was deterrence and there she did admit  that one should should probably have  done more on the military side yeah when  she says one should have done more on  the military side i mean she's been  criticized for not  being tougher with putin while she was  in power for 16 years i mean did she  take any responsibility for  the threat to european security that we  now face  well um yes and no um she says yes of  course it was also her responsibility to  build an architecture that would contain  russia and that's what she felt she had  done um her sense was that by not  allowing and let's be very concrete here  ukraine to join nato earlier not  allowing that path towards nato that  former u.s president bush wanted for  instance and she put fear still feels  today and that's why she says she's  resting easy on this that she prevented  something terrible from happening  earlier and that at the very least the  minsk agreement after the annexation of  crimea in 2014 had bought ukraine time a  country which she describes as being a  very different country from seven years  ago and that it is much more of a stable  democracy and that partly is also why  vladimir putin sees it as such a threat  let's just take a listen to what she had  to say on that fundamental difference  between putin and her  it was clear that we had a big  disagreement  and our positions kept growing apart  for all intents and purposes  and looking back  we haven't been able to effectively put  an end to the cold war  there's always been the issue of russia  i don't agree with putin to make it  perfectly clear  but we've also not been able to forge a  security architecture that could have  kept this from happening  that's something to think about  and it's something historians will  certainly ponder  but i want to make it clear so that  there's no  misunderstanding there is no justifying  the attack on ukraine that took place on  february 24th  it is a brutal attack flying in the face  of human rights for which there is no  excuse  so clearly the german class is still  doing quite a lot of thinking on what  she could have done differently but not  really departing from that course that  she's taking towards vladimir putin yeah  michaela there are critics of the former  chancellor calling for a reckoning with  her legacy  do you think  tonight's interview there on stage will  will that  satisfy her critics  no clearly not um also and we know that  uh the ukrainian ambassador mel nick  kieran berlin contacted the interviewer  beforehand and the very question was why  didn't you allow us to join nato the  question he has for germany continuously  is why are you not sending more weapons  and i think that criticism won't stop  but what she did tonight is explain that  she did have a strategy to find a way of  coexistence and notably the lack of  european unity is something she felt was  in the way of having a much more stable  security architecture that could have  potentially contained russia  yeah and on that point she faced that  problem many many times that is for sure  our chief political editor mikhail  kufner tonight here in berlin michelle  thank you  all right i want to pull into the chief  europe correspondent at politico matthew  karnich he joins me from right here in  berlin tonight matthew it's good to see  you again  you've written in very clear and direct  language pointing the finger at angela  merkel and accusing her as being one of  putin's useful idiots while she was in  power  she took questions tonight for the first  time here  will her answers will they satisfy her  critics  i don't think so because i think what  she was trying to do this evening was  what some of us expected which was to  effectively defend her record which is  which is her right of course but if you  look back over the trajectory  of the 15 plus years that she was  chancellor you could see really a  pattern that i think someone in her  position really should have recognized  when it came to russia and when it came  to vladimir putin in particular  if you look at the invasion of georgia  in august of 2008 for example  followed by  the  annexation of crimea in 2014 the war in  dombas also of 2014  what russia did in syria and so forth  and throughout all of this miracle's  response was to really seek out putin  and to try and engage him through what  she called dialogue thinking that that  would be the answer to this aggression  and i think in retrospect  that policy clearly didn't work and as  merkel said today ultimately will be  historians who will  decide whether  her policies were the right ones or not  and i think you know that the  from my point of view that that the the  judgment is already pretty clear  but if she were to say i was wrong about  putin i should have pursued a different  policy  i do carry responsibility here i mean  what difference would that make for  the current government  i think it would make a huge difference  to be honest and let's be clear she  didn't say that tonight she said in fact  the opposite of that she said well this  was just sort of diplomacy and diplomacy  these are the sorts of things one needs  to do i think if she were to come  forward and say  i was wrong we were wrong from the  beginning and let's not forget that in  2008 before the invasion of georgia that  i mentioned in  may of 2008  merkel led a push to keep ukraine and  georgia out of nato this was something  that merkel really wanted to prevent and  it was something that the united states  of america at the time under george w  bush a president who's not really  thought well of in large  parts of  europe because of what happened in iraq  but but he was pushing for nato  enlargement to include ukraine and  georgia merkel was very much against  this and prevented it and there are a  lot of diplomats and other experts who  look at that decision now and say well  that really would have made a difference  today because if ukraine which was a  different country at the time obviously  but if they were under the  nuclear umbrella of nato this wouldn't  have happened so i think if she were to  have come forth today  and said look this was  a failed policy from the beginning it  would make a difference for the current  government because it's important to  remember this wasn't just miracles  policy this was german policy and it  wasn't controversial in germany it was  shared across the political landscape  for the past 15 years  there is  a a reckoning if you will with miracles  time in power i mean we're talking about  almost two decades here we're reporting  on it you're writing about it  but we still remain outliers i think  when we're talking about  the entirety of of the media but  especially here in germany will  tonight's comments her breaking her  silence if you will do you think that  that will maybe open up the reckoning  more so here  i think unfortunately within germany i  don't i don't think it will because i i  also feel that the  the media here especially the  public media which is so  influential in germany was complicit in  all of this because they bought this  line  basically  hook line and sinker  as as we'd say in the united states  that this was the right policy that  engaging putin was ultimately going to  bring about  the kind of  results that we wanted a partnership  that the west was seeking with him so i  don't think that her statements today  are going to make any difference on on  that score in fact i think it's going to  just harden this impression that this  was the right policy at the time it  didn't work out  but  you know this is the this was the the  sort of these were the cards we were  dealt at the time and we played them as  best we could  the chief europe correspondent at  politico matthew karnichini joining us  tonight interesting insights matthew we  appreciate it thank you  thank you
 

